artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a repairable system consisting of two dissimilar units that may operate in three modes (normal, partial failure, total failure), subject to random checks. Let the state of the system at time t be described by the joint probability densities (with x denoting the elapsed repair/check time as a supplementary variable):

p_{0,0,k}(x,t)  (k=0,1 indicating normal operation with random-check status),
p_{1,0,0}(x,t), p_{0,1,0}(x,t), p_{1,1,0}(x,t), p_{0,1,1}(x,t), p_{1,0,1}(x,t),
p_{0,2,0}(x,t), p_{2,0,0}(x,t), p_{1,1,1}(x,t), p_{1,2,0}(x,t), p_{2,1,0}(x,t),

where the first two subscripts denote the failure level (0=normal, 1=partial, 2=total) of unit 1 and unit 2 respectively, and the third subscript records the random-check status. Assume the parameters:

- λ_{1,0}: failure rate of unit 1 from normal to partial failure;
- λ_{2,0}: failure rate of unit 1 from partial to total failure;
- λ_{0,1}: failure rate of unit 2 from normal to partial failure;
- λ_{0,2}: failure rate of unit 2 from partial to total failure;
- α(x) ≥ 0: random-check rate satisfying ∫_0^∞ α(x) dx = ∞;
- μ_{i,0}(x), μ_{0,i}(x): repair rates for unit 1 or unit 2 alone (i=1,2);
- μ_{i,j}(x): repair rate when both units require repair.

The state probabilities satisfy the standard supplementary-variable system of coupled first-order partial differential equations of Kolmogorov type, with boundary conditions at x=0 expressing transitions into states under repair/check, and an initial condition placing all probability mass in the fully operational state at t=0.

Tasks:
(a) Prove that this PDE system is well posed: namely, show existence, uniqueness, and continuous dependence on initial data of a nonnegative time-dependent solution in an appropriate Banach space (e.g., L^1).
(b) Determine the asymptotic behavior of the time-dependent solution as t → ∞, including whether it converges strongly to the steady-state solution of the corresponding stationary problem.

